                "text": "I'm testing whether the rule is picking the smallest shape by cell count, but the examples don't consistently fit that pattern\u2014checking if it's instead about picking the uniquely-shaped or asymmetric one.\n\nMaybe it's a staircase shape versus rectangles/L-shapes \u2014 7 and the two 4s look staircase-like while others are rectangular or L-shaped. Or perhaps it's about diagonal symmetry: 7 and one 4 are symmetric across the main diagonal, though that doesn't hold cleanly for the other 4.\n\nLet me consider whether the answer relates to shape size, or to one shape being a scaled/mirrored version of another \u2014 checking if the 4 in example 2 is just the 3-shape doubled or mirrored, but the row patterns don't quite match. I should think about position instead, like which shape sits closest to the bottom-left corner.\n\nThen I check the positions of chosen outputs across examples but they don't share a consistent corner. Instead I notice the count of filled cells relative to bounding box area might matter \u2014 testing the ratio of filled cells to bounding box size for each shape, and it seems like the selected shape consistently has a 3/4 fill ratio, which fits example 1's 7-shape and example 3's 4-shape, and example 2's 4-shape at 6 out of 8 cells also gives 3/4. I'm now verifying this against the other shapes in example 2 to see if their ratios differ.\n\n",
